course: history
lesson: 12
title: Ancient Greece: democracy, the Olympics, myths and thinkers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: About 2,500 years ago the citizens of Athens met on a hill and voted on laws, war and peace themselves. They called it democracy, rule by the people, though women, slaves and foreigners could not vote.
explain: Think of a class vote on where to go for the school trip: everyone with a vote has a say, and the side with more hands wins. Athens did that for a whole city, and could even vote to send a too-powerful politician away for ten years.
explain: The Greeks also gave us the Olympic Games, the theatre, and a world of myths about Zeus, Athena and heroes like Heracles, which were real religion to them, not just stories like Percy Jackson.
explain: Their thinkers asked big questions: Socrates by asking question after question, Aristotle by studying everything, Archimedes in his bath, and Hippocrates by teaching that illness has natural causes. Then Alexander the Great carried Greek ideas from Egypt to India.
fact: Democracy means rule by the people; in Athens, about 2,500 years ago, citizens met on a hill to vote on laws and on war and peace.
fact: In Athens women, slaves and foreigners could not vote.
fact: Once a year Athenians could vote to send a too-powerful politician away for ten years by scratching his name on a bit of broken pottery called an ostrakon; this is called ostracism.
fact: The Olympic Games began in 776 BC at Olympia in honour of Zeus, with running, wrestling, the discus and chariot races, and wars paused so people could travel safely.
fact: Ancient Olympic winners got a crown of olive leaves, not gold medals, and only men could compete; the modern Olympics began in 1896.
fact: The Greeks believed in many gods living on Mount Olympus, such as Zeus the king, Hera, Poseidon of the sea and Athena of wisdom, who behaved a lot like quarrelsome humans; the wild god Pan was said to fill people with sudden fear, which gave us the word panic.
fact: Greek myths tell of heroes like Heracles with his twelve labours, Perseus and Medusa, and Theseus and the Minotaur; modern films and books, like Disney's Hercules or Percy Jackson, change these old myths a lot to make new stories.
fact: The Greeks invented theatre, with comedies and tragedies performed in great open-air theatres by actors wearing masks.
fact: Socrates taught by asking questions about what is good, fair and true, which often showed that important people knew less than they thought, and he was put on trial and sentenced to death for upsetting powerful people.
fact: Plato, Socrates' pupil, wrote about the ideal city; Aristotle, Plato's pupil, studied everything from animals to poetry and logic.
fact: Pythagoras studied triangles, Archimedes discovered how things float and shouted Eureka, and Eratosthenes measured the size of the Earth with shadows.
fact: Hippocrates, called the father of medicine, taught that illness has natural causes, not punishments from the gods.
fact: Alexander of Macedon, taught by Aristotle, conquered a huge empire from Greece to Egypt and India before he died at 32, and founded Alexandria in Egypt.
exercise: What does democracy mean? | Rule by the people | Rule by one king | Rule by the army | Rule by the gods
exercise: Who could NOT vote in ancient Athens? | Women, slaves and foreigners | Men whose parents were born in Athens | Rich citizens | Old citizens
exercise: What was an ostrakon? | A broken bit of pottery used as a voting slip | A Greek gold coin with an owl on it | A warship with oars | A mask worn by actors on stage
exercise: What did ostracism do to a too-powerful politician? | Sent him away for ten years | Made him king for life | Gave him a gold medal | Put him in the theatre
exercise: When did the ancient Olympic Games begin? | 776 BC | 1896 AD | 221 BC | 490 BC
exercise: In honour of which god were the Olympics held? | Zeus | Poseidon | Thor | Anubis
exercise: What did an ancient Olympic winner receive? | A crown of olive leaves | A gold medal | A new house in Athens | A chariot made of silver
exercise: What happened to wars during the ancient Olympics? | They paused so people could travel safely | They got bigger and louder every four years | They moved inside the stadium | Nothing, they went on as usual
exercise: Which god ruled the sea? | Poseidon | Zeus | Athena | Apollo
exercise: Which hero had twelve labours? | Heracles | Perseus | Odysseus | Theseus
exercise: What did Greek actors wear on stage? | Masks | Helmets with horns | Nothing on their faces | Paper crowns
exercise: How did Socrates teach? | By asking questions | By writing long books | By giving orders | By drawing pictures
exercise: Who was Aristotle's teacher? | Plato | Socrates' son | Alexander | Homer
exercise: What did Archimedes discover in his bath? | How things float | That the Earth spins | How to make paper | The number zero and how to use it
exercise: What did Hippocrates teach about illness? | It has natural causes | It is a punishment from the gods | It comes from bad dreams | It is caused by the Moon only
exercise: Who taught Alexander the Great? | Aristotle | Socrates | Pythagoras | Leonidas
exercise: Which city did Alexander found in Egypt? | Alexandria | Athens | Babylon | Troy
test: mate says ancient Olympic winners got gold medals like now. did they? | No, they got a crown of olive leaves | Yes, gold, silver and bronze, just like now | Yes, and a new chariot and a big house | No, they got nothing at all, not even a prize
test: Your sister says everyone in Athens could vote. Is she right? | No, women, slaves and foreigners could not | Yes, every single person living in the city | Yes, even children voted | No, only the king voted
test: Disney's Hercules is great fun. What is fair to say? | It changes almost everything in the Greek myth | It follows the old Greek myth exactly, scene by scene | It is a true history film | Hercules was a Roman emperor
test: In Percy Jackson the gods are like modern heroes. What were they for the Greeks? | Real gods to worship | Only cartoon characters | Ordinary kings of Athens | Aliens from outer space
test: If Athenians feared a politician might make himself king, what could they do? | Vote to ostracise him for ten years | Make him their king at once, to keep the peace | Give him an olive crown | Nothing, voting was banned
test: Why might powerful people have been upset by Socrates' questions? | His questions showed they did not know as much as they thought | He shouted at them in the street | He stole their money and their gold | He spoke only Persian, so nobody could understand him
test: A doctor says your fever is a punishment from Zeus. What would Hippocrates say? | Look for the natural cause of the illness | Pray to Zeus and do nothing else at all | Zeus is right, as always | Fevers are caused by sitting too long in the theatre
test: Why do we still use the word panic? | It comes from the Greek god Pan | It is the name of an Olympic sport | It was a Roman city | It means olive oil
test: Why did the Olympics pause wars? | So athletes and fans could travel safely | Because all the soldiers were running in the races | Because Zeus banned swords for ever | Because it was winter
test: ok so how did Eratosthenes measure the earth, he didn't have a plane? | He used shadows and some clever maths | He walked all the way round the world | He asked a god | He sailed around it in a year and counted the days
test: Alexander spread Greek language and ideas from Egypt to India. Which is true? | He died young, at 32 | He lived to 100 | He was a Roman | He never left Greece
exam: Identify the definition of the term democracy as understood in classical Athens. | Government by the assembled citizens | Government by a hereditary monarch and his sons | Government by the priests of Zeus | Government by the army alone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the reward conferred upon victors at the ancient Olympic Games. | A wreath of olive leaves | A gold medal | A sum of silver coins | A chariot of bronze
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the teaching of Hippocrates in respect of disease? | Disease arises from natural causes | Disease is a punishment inflicted by the gods | Disease is transmitted by the theatre | Disease is caused by the phases of the Moon
exam: Ascertain the persons excluded from voting in the assembly of Athens. | Women, the enslaved and foreigners | Men born of Athenian parents | Citizens of advanced age | The wealthy citizens alone
